WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Hold the spacebar, speak normally, and
clawed code transcribes every

2
00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.680
word you say.

3
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.040
That half of the loop works beautifully,
but the reply?

4
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.120
That always comes back if text.

5
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.520
You speak to the machine and it types back
in

6
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.640
dense, silent blocks on a dark terminal
screen.

7
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.280
It forces a constant mental gear shift.

8
00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.560
You're trying to work through a complex
architecture verbally, but

9
00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.760
then you have to pause, stop talking, and
parse through

10
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.040
markdown diffs to see what the assistant
actually thinks.

11
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.680
The exchange is entirely asymmetrical.

12
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.640
The natural rhythm of human conversation
hits a wall the

13
00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.800
moment the AI takes its turn.

14
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.120
Afterwards was built to close this loop.

15
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.160
It's an open source tool that runs
offline, directly on

16
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.680
your machine. It hooks into clawed code,
catches the text

17
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.160
response before you even read it, and
routes it to

18
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.000
a local text-to-speech server.

19
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.400
The result plays straight through your
speakers.

20
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.760
Because the entire pipeline sits right on
your hardware, zero

21
00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.200
data leaves the machine.

22
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:01.240
There are no API calls to third-party
servers, meaning your

23
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.600
proprietary code base stays entirely
local.

24
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.360
When the AI actually talks back, you stop
reading output

25
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.200
logs. Code review turns into an actual
dialogue.

26
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.480
Historically, high-quality voice synthesis
meant relying on heavy cloud infrastructure.

27
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:21.480
You had to accept network latency,
recurring API costs, and

28
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.880
sending your raw data over the internet.

29
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.360
The math changes with Apple Silicon.

30
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.480
Devices like the M1 chip share their RAM
between the

31
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:34.040
CPU and GPU, a structure called unified
memory, which allows

32
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.320
consumer laptops to hold large models
locally.

33
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.400
Afterwards, leverages this by running a
text-to-speech engine called Quen3

34
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.200
TTS. It sits on Apple's MLX framework,
designed specifically to

35
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.760
optimize machine learning on their
silicon.

36
00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.520
This setup relies on a 0.6 billion
parameter model with

37
00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:55.000
8-bit quantization, shrinking the model
size.

38
00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.160
It only takes up 6 gigabytes of memory at
peak

39
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.880
load. Between the unified hardware
architecture and the compressed weights,

40
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.920
generating fluid, human-sounding speech
offline is now completely realistic for

41
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.520
a standard MacBook Air.

42
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.720
The pipeline relies on a stop hook, a
simple script

43
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.240
that fires the exact moment clawed code
finishes a thought.

44
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.160
It automatically strips out markdown
formatting, preventing the AI from

45
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.920
pronouncing code syntax.

46
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.200
Those clean text lines drop into a
background queue.

47
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.880
A worker process handles them serially,
locking the directory so

48
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.760
you never get two audio files talking over
each other.

49
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.520
From there, the engine instantly maps the
text onto 17

50
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.080
distinct pre-loaded cloned voices, giving
your codebase a variety of

51
00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.160
human personalities to choose from.

52
00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.560
You can have error logs explained in the
cadence of

53
00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.840
Kate Blanchette, or choose Alan Rickman to
walk you through

54
00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.520
a complex security review.

55
00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:53.560
Each voice requires zero fine-tuning and
no duplicate models.

56
00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:57.400
The system extracts the speaker's tone
from a tiny 700

57
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.000
kilobyte audio clip at the exact moment of
inference.

58
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.680
Loading one voice uses the exact same
memory overhead as

59
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.360
loading all 17.

60
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.800
You get a wide variety of vocal ranges and
temperaments,

61
00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:12.600
and your laptop's performance doesn't take
a hit.

62
00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.120
This entire sequence takes about 20
seconds to generate a

63
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:20.600
standard response. That latency means it
isn't built for rapid-fire

64
00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.440
trial and error debugging where you need
instant visual feedback.

65
00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.240
Where it excels is deep comprehension.

66
00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.800
You can keep your eyes locked on the
specific logic

67
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.440
of your code base while the assistant
walks you through

68
00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.920
an architectural refactor aloud.

69
00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.760
You can even assign specific personalities
to specific tasks.

70
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:42.840
Just drop a .afterwards file into a
project's root repository,

71
00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.520
and the system automatically matches the
code base to a

72
00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.760
designated voice. By offloading the
explanation to your ears, your

73
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.640
eyes are free to trace the structure of
the code.

74
00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.000
It creates a parallel stream of
information that makes absorbing

75
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.560
dense concepts noticeably smoother.

76
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.000
The server running this audio wasn't
originally designed for clawed

77
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.040
code. It was built to voice a physical
robot companion

78
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.520
named Spark. Because the components are
entirely modular, connecting them

79
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.720
took nothing more than a single HTTP
endpoint.

80
00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:16.480
The same local API handling a robot's
speech simply points

81
00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.480
to a coding assistant instead.

82
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.160
That identical infrastructure can be
dropped into almost anything.

83
00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.960
One local machine can use the same loaded
model to

84
00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.640
read out documentation or narrate
notifications for a home automation

85
00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.840
server. Consumer hardware has caught up to
the demands of

86
00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.960
real-time voice generation, and the open
source models to run

87
00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:36.560
it are freely available.

88
00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.440
Developers now have the raw materials to
construct secure, highly

89
00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:44.600
responsive sensory environments entirely
under their own roof.

